---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CornerFrame from '../components/ornaments/CornerFrame.astro';

const languages = await getCollection('languages');
const terms = await getCollection('terms');
const languageCount = languages.filter((l) => !l.data.isMoc).length;
const termCount = terms.filter((t) => !t.data.isMoc).length;
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="What Learnex is and how it's built.">
  <div class="about-page">
    <CornerFrame class="page-leaf">
      <div class="page-leaf-inner">
        <header class="about-header">
          <h1>About Learnex</h1>
        </header>

        <div class="vault-prose drop-cap">
          <p>
            Learnex is a personal Obsidian vault, rendered as a site. Every page here, all
            {languageCount + termCount} of them, is a plain markdown file living in a folder one
            level above this project. Nothing was copied in or exported. A custom loader reads
            the vault directly at build time, so editing a note in Obsidian and rebuilding is the
            entire publishing process.
          </p>

          <h2>How it's built</h2>
          <p>
            The site is Astro, statically generated, with no server and no database. A Content
            Layer loader walks two folders in the vault, "Programming Languages" and "Terms and
            Knowledge", parses each note's frontmatter, and rewrites every <code>[[wikilink]]</code>
            into a real route by matching titles across the whole vault. Search is
            <a href="https://pagefind.app" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pagefind</a>, indexed
            after the production build, which is why search only works once you've run
            <code>npm run build</code> and not in a plain dev server.
          </p>
          <p>
            The <a href="/graph">knowledge graph</a> and the "referenced by" list at the foot of
            every note are the same underlying data, just read in opposite directions: one is a
            note's outgoing links, the other is everyone else's incoming links to it. Nothing is
            computed twice.
          </p>

          <h2>Review</h2>
          <p>
            The <a href="/review">flashcard deck</a> is a small SM-2 flavored spaced-repetition
            scheduler, not a full SuperMemo implementation, running entirely in your browser's
            local storage. Cards are drawn from the "Definition:" line every note opens with. No
            question was hand-authored separately from the notes themselves; sharpen a note's
            definition and its card sharpens with it.
          </p>

          <h2>What's in it</h2>
          <p>
            Right now the vault holds {languageCount} programming language notes and {termCount}
            term notes across the domains listed on the <a href="/terms">Terms &amp; Knowledge</a>
            page. Both numbers change as the vault grows, since this page reads them the same way
            every other page does: live, from the vault, at build time.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </CornerFrame>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-page {
    max-width: 52rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2.5rem 2rem 6rem;
  }
  .page-leaf-inner {
    padding: 3.5rem 2.5rem;
  }
  .about-header {
    max-width: 40rem;
    margin: 0 auto 1.75rem;
  }
  .about-header h1 {
    font-size: 2.15rem;
    margin: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .about-page {
      padding: 1.25rem 1rem 3rem;
    }
    .page-leaf-inner {
      padding: 1.75rem 1.35rem;
    }
    .about-header h1 {
      font-size: 1.6rem;
    }
  }
</style>
